I wrote `polyglot/main.rs`. I built it with both `rustc` and `g++ -x c++`, and both programs printed the same results: N=0 → 1, 1 → 1, 2 → 2, 3 → 3, 10 → 89, 50 → 20365011074, 90 → 4660046610375530309.

```rust
// \
fn main() { let n: u64 = std::env::args().nth(1).expect("usage: main N").trim().parse().expect("N must be a non-negative integer"); let (mut a, mut b) = (1u64, 1u64); for _ in 0..n { let t = a.wrapping_add(b); a = b; b = t; } println!("{}", a); } /*
#include <cstdio>
#include <cstdlib>
int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    if (argc < 2) { std::fprintf(stderr, "usage: main N\n"); return 1; }
    unsigned long long n = std::strtoull(argv[1], nullptr, 10);
    unsigned long long a = 1, b = 1;
    for (unsigned long long i = 0; i < n; ++i) { unsigned long long t = a + b; a = b; b = t; }
    std::printf("%llu\n", a);
    return 0;
}
// */
```

**How it works:**
- **C++:** A backslash at the end of a `//` comment carries the comment onto the next line, so g++ ignores the whole Rust line. It then compiles the C++ code. The final `// */` is just a normal comment.
- **Rust:** The backslash has no special meaning, so the first line is just a comment and the Rust `fn main` is compiled. The `/*` at the end of that line starts a block comment that hides all the C++ code until the `*/` on the last line.

**Limits:**
- Both versions use 64-bit unsigned numbers, so results are only correct up to N=92. Above that, the number is too large and wraps around to a wrong value.
- `rustc` puts the executable in the directory you run it from, not next to the source file. Your command runs `polyglot/main`, so either run `rustc` from inside `polyglot/` or add `-o /tmp/obench-cell-ts1gxtmy/bench_opencode_polyglot-rust-c_f8uowouk/polyglot/main`.
